{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015GUwrp8oL4MdQ3R66DR29s","type":"tool_result","content":"19\tpub struct DeviceBuffer<T> {\n20\t    ptr: *mut T,\n21\t    len: usize,\n22\t}\n23\t\n24\t/// A struct that packs a pointer with a size in bytes to pass on CUDA.\n25\t/// It holds `*const c_void` for being a universal simple type that can be read by CUDA.\n26\t/// Since it is hard to enforce immutability preservation, it just holds `*const`,\n27\t/// but has two separate constructors for more robustness from the usage perspective.\n28\t/// This is essentially a [DeviceBuffer] but without owning the data.\n29\t#[repr(C)]\n30\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone)]\n31\tpub struct DeviceBufferView {\n32\t    pub ptr: *const c_void,\n33\t    pub size: usize,\n34\t}\n35\t\n36\tunsafe impl<T> Send for DeviceBuffer<T> {}\n37\tunsafe impl<T> Sync for DeviceBuffer<T> {}\n38\t\n39\timpl<T> DeviceBuffer<T> {\n40\t    /// Creates an \"empty\" DeviceBuffer with a null pointer and zero length.\n41\t    #[allow(clippy::new_without_default)]\n42\t    pub fn new() -> Self {\n43\t        DeviceBuffer {\n44\t            ptr: ptr::null_mut(),\n45\t            len: 0,\n46\t        }\n47\t    }\n48\t\n49\t    /// # Safety\n50\t    /// - The caller must ensure that the pointer `ptr` is valid for `len` elements of type `T` in\n51\t    ///   device memory.\n52\t    /// - Dropping the constructed buffer will attempt to free the memory. As such, `ptr` must\n53\t    ///   either have been allocated by the internal memory manager (VPMM) or the caller must use\n54\t    ///   `ManuallyDrop` to prevent double-free.\n55\t    pub unsafe fn from_raw_parts(ptr: *mut T, len: usize) -> Self {\n56\t        DeviceBuffer { ptr, len }\n57\t    }\n58\t\n59\t    /// Allocate device memory for `len` elements of type `T`.\n60\t    pub fn with_capacity(len: usize) -> Self {\n61\t        tracing::debug!(\n62\t            \"Creating device buffer of size {} (sizeof type = {})\",\n63\t     