{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QTsiUrQ29EQyxfe2tUQLXM","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Task: Defer Round 0 D2H Synchronization\n2\t\n3\tTask name: 2026-04-10-1933-defer-round0-d2h-sync\n4\t\n5\t## Problem Statement\n6\t\n7\tRound 0 of the constraint evaluation phase is the single largest contributor to the GPU prover's failure to scale with APC count. As APCs increase from 0 to 300, AIR instances grow from 99 to 623, but Round 0 time *increases* from 178ms to 663ms (3.7x worse), despite total computational work (cells, constraint instances, bus interaction messages) all decreasing by >2x.\n8\t\n9\tProfiling data from nsight shows that APC 300 launches ~935 Round 0 kernels (`zerocheck_ntt_evaluate_constraints_*` + `logup_r0_ntt_eval_interactions_*`), each followed by a **blocking D2H transfer and CPU-side processing** before the next kernel can launch. This sequential pattern is in `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:730-880`.\n10\t\n11\tThe per-AIR loop structure is:\n12\t```\n13\tfor each AIR instance (623 total for APC 300):\n14\t    1. Launch zerocheck kernel on GPU\n15\t    2. BLOCKING D2H transfer (to_host() at line 792)\n16\t    3. CPU-side IDFT computation\n17\t    4. Launch logup kernel on GPU\n18\t    5. BLOCKING D2H transfer (to_host() at line 848)\n19\t    6. CPU-side polynomial construction\n20\t```\n21\t\n22\tThis creates 1,246 blocking synchronization points. Between each sync, the GPU sits idle while the CPU processes results. The result buffers are tiny (< 1KB each — just a few extension field elements per coset evaluation), so the overhead comes entirely from the synchronization and pipeline stalls, not from data transfer volume.\n23\t\n24\tCritically, **all loop iterations are independent**: the zerocheck and logup kernels for AIR[i] do not depend on results from any other AIR. The only shared read-only data are `d_lambda_pows` and `eq_xi_tree`.\n25\t\n26\t## Proposed Approach\n27\t\n28\tRestructure the Round 0 per-AIR loop in `LogupZerocheckGpu::batch_constraints_round0()` (`crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:730-880`) into two phases:\n29\t\n30\t**Phase 1 — GPU Launch Phase**: Iterate over all AIR instances, launching both zerocheck and logup kernels for each, without any intermediate D2H transfers. Collect the GPU result buffers (`DeviceBuffer`) into vectors. The kernels execute sequentially on the default CUDA stream (which is correct and safe), but the CPU doesn't block waiting for each one.\n31\t\n32\t```rust\n33\tlet mut zc_results: Vec<(usize, DeviceBuffer<EF>, usize, ...)> = Vec::new();\n34\tlet mut logup_results: Vec<(usize, DeviceBuffer<FracExt>, usize, ...)> = Vec::new();\n35\t\n36\tfor (trace_idx, ...) in ctx.per_trace... {\n37\t    // Same setup as before (single_pk, d_main_parts, etc.)\n38\t    \n39\t    let sum_buffer = evaluate_round0_constraints_gpu(...)?;\n40\t    zc_results.push((trace_idx, sum_buffer, num_cosets_zc, ...));\n41\t    \n42\t    let sum = evaluate_round0_interactions_gpu(...)?;\n43\t    logup_results.push((trace_idx, sum, num_cosets_logup, ...));\n44\t}\n45\t```\n46\t\n47\t**Phase 2 — CPU Processing Phase**: After all kernels are queued, process all results. The first `to_host()` call will implicitly synchronize the stream (waiting for all queued kernels to complete), and subsequent `to_host()` calls will be instant since the data is already computed.\n48\t\n49\t```rust\n50\tfor (trace_idx, sum_buffer, num_cosets_zc, ...) in zc_results {\n51\t    if !sum_buffer.is_empty() {\n52\t        let q_evals = sum_buffer.to_host()?;\n53\t        // CPU IDFT and polynomial construction (unchanged)\n54\t        batch_sp_poly[2 * num_present_airs + trace_idx] = ...;\n55\t    }\n56\t}\n57\t\n58\tfor (trace_idx, sum, num_cosets_logup, ...) in logup_results {\n59\t    if !sum.is_empty() {\n60\t        let evals = sum.to_host()?;\n61\t        // CPU polynomial construction (unchanged)\n62\t        batch_sp_poly[2 * trace_idx] = ...;\n63\t        batch_sp_poly[2 * trace_idx + 1] = ...;\n64\t    }\n65\t}\n66\t```\n67\t\n68\tKey implementation details:\n69\t- `d_main_parts` (per-AIR GPU pointer arrays) must be collected to outlive Phase 1\n70\t- Result buffers are tiny (< 1KB each), so holding 623 simultaneously uses negligible GPU memory (~600KB total)\n71\t- No CUDA kernel changes are needed — only Rust-side loop restructuring\n72\t- The GPU still executes kernels sequentially on the default stream, but without host-side pipeline stalls between launches\n73\t\n74\tOptionally, the CPU processing in Phase 2 can be parallelized using rayon (`par_iter`) since each AIR's IDFT is independent, further reducing CPU-side time.\n75\t\n76\t## Expected Outcome\n77\t\n78\t- **APC 300 Round 0**: 663ms -> ~350-450ms (35-47% reduction)\n79\t  - Eliminating 1,246 sync points removes ~200-300ms of pipeline stall overhead\n80\t  - Optionally parallelizing CPU IDFTs with rayon saves additional ~50-100ms\n81\t- **APC 0 Round 0**: 178ms -> ~130-160ms (10-27% reduction, less impact due to fewer instances)\n82\t- **STARK excl trace (APC 300)**: 2478ms -> ~2180-2280ms (8-12% improvement)\n83\t- **Scaling ratio** (APC 300 / APC 0 STARK excl trace): improves from 1.15 toward ~1.05\n84\t\n85\tThis is the first step toward proper scaling. Subsequent optimizations (stacked reduction batching, multi-stream pipelining) can compound on this improvement.\n86\t\n87\t## Success Criteria\n88\t\n89\t1. **Primary**: STARK excl trace for APC 300 decreases by at least 150ms (from 2478ms baseline)\n90\t2. **Secondary**: Round 0 time for APC 300 decreases by at least 30% (from 663ms to <465ms)\n91\t3. **Scaling**: The ratio (APC 300 STARK excl trace) / (APC 0 STARK excl trace) improves (currently 1.15, target < 1.10)\n92\t4. **Correctness**: All existing tests pass, proof verification succeeds for all APC configurations\n93\t\n94\t## Considered Alternatives\n95\t\n96\t1. **Batch stacked reduction degenerate kernels** (ranked #2): Replace 10,348 single-block degenerate kernel launches with ~17 batched launches (one per round). Expected ~100-150ms savings on Stacked Reduction (0.31s -> ~0.16-0.20s). This is a good follow-up task but has lower impact (targets +195ms anti-scaling vs Round 0's +485ms) and requires CUDA kernel changes.\n97\t\n98\t2. **Multi-stream Round 0 with CPU/GPU overlap** (ranked #3): Use multiple CUDA streams to overlap GPU execution with CPU IDFT processing. Higher potential (~300-400ms savings) but significantly more complex — requires async D2H transfers, stream management, and event-based synchronization. Better suited as a follow-up after the simpler deferred-sync approach proves the benefit.\n99\t\n100\t3. **Fuse Round 0 kernels across AIR instances** (ranked #4): Launch a single large kernel that processes multiple AIRs simultaneously. High potential but difficult because each AIR has different constraint structures (different proving keys, different numbers of constraints and interactions). The data-driven kernel would be complex to implement correctly.\n101\t\n102\t4. **Parallelize CPU IDFT with rayon** (ranked #5 standalone, but can be combined with #1): Wrap the CPU-side IDFT processing in `rayon::par_iter`. Simple and effective (~50-100ms savings) but smaller standalone impact. Included as an optional enhancement in the proposed approach.\n103\t\n104\tThe deferred D2H sync approach was chosen over these alternatives because it provides the highest (impact x probability / complexity) ratio: it targets the largest bottleneck (+485ms), requires only Rust-side changes (no CUDA kernel modifications), has minimal risk (loop iterations are provably independent), and establishes the foundation for future streaming optimizations.\n105\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01A4bJ2faf2icpueuGAwcM3V","session_id":"ebef63bf-f161-4614-a323-d6b5d42117d0","uuid":"136856ea-3e24-42ba-9e82-d22e20adf53b","timestamp":"2026-04-10T17:45:48.491Z"}
